### Detailed Solution


### Step 1: Relate displacement current to the rate of change of potential difference

The displacement current \(I_d\) in a parallel plate capacitor is given by:
I_d = C\frac{dV}{dt}
Where \(C\) is the capacitance and \(\frac{dV}{dt}\) is the rate of change of potential difference. We need to find \(\frac{dV}{dt}\).
\frac{dV}{dt} = \frac{I_d}{C}

### Step 2: Substitute the given values and calculate

Given values:
• Capacitance \(C = 2.5 \mu F = 2.5 \times 10^{-6} F\)
• Displacement current \(I_d = 0.25 mA = 0.25 \times 10^{-3} A\)
Substitute these values into the equation:
\frac{dV}{dt} = \frac{0.25\times 10^{-3}}{2.5\times 10^{-6}}
\frac{dV}{dt} = \frac{0.25}{2.5} \times 10^{(-3 - (-6))}
\frac{dV}{dt} = 0.1 \times 10^{3}
\frac{dV}{dt} = 100 \text{ Vs}^{-1}

